					<p class="body1" align="left"><span class="body1 style73"> <h2>News archive</h2><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-14 | Tuesday</font></strong><br><br><div align="left"><div style="text-align: center"><img src="http://a578.ac-images.myspacecdn.com/images01/36/l_ff8608533f158217e8b33d68de514ac1.jpg" alt="" width="322" height="450" /><br /><br /><div align="left"><div>Just wanted to give a heads up that there&#39;s <strong>ONE DAY</strong> left to vote for the homie <strong>Angela Dy</strong> for <strong>Seattle&#39;s Poet Populist</strong>. Here&#39;s the link:<br /><br /></div><div><a href="http://www.seattlepoetpopulist.org/vote.htm" target="_blank" onclick="return top.js.OpenExtLink(window,event,this)">http://www.seattlepoetpopulist.org/vote.htm</a></div><p><strong>Angela Martinez Dy</strong>,
poet, spoken word artist and emcee with <strong>isangmahal arts kollective</strong>
roots. Performance poet since fourteen, and founding member, mentor and
educator for youth poetry organization <strong>Youth Speaks Seattle</strong>, Angela
became its Program Director in 2005. She has been integral in bringing
young people&#39;s voices to the forefront of the poetry community in
Seattle and nationwide. She teaches poetry and performance to youth and
organizes an annual youth slam series, culminating in a team
representing Seattle at the <strong>National Youth Poetry Festival</strong>. This past
June, she was responsible for the production of the sold-out <strong>Seattle
Youth Poetry Grand Slam</strong>.</p><p>Spread the word...and more importantly, <em>VOTE</em>!</p><p>Peace<br />geo and daps</p></div></div></div><p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-13 | Monday</font></strong><br><br><div style="text-align: center"><img src="http://a434.ac-images.myspacecdn.com/images01/127/l_de25f3a777b35ac6da7aedc50fbae099.jpg" alt="" width="363" height="363" /></div><br /><br />What&#39;s good yall? If you&#39;re looking for breath of fresh air in this music game, then let me hip yall to my guy <strong>J.Pinder</strong>, yet another rhyme-slinger from <strong>Sportn&#39; Life Records</strong>. He&#39;s got a crazy buzz in the Seattle hip-hop scene and is breaking off a little appetizer of his album THIS FRIDAY in the form of the mixtape &quot;<u><strong>Backpack Wax</strong></u>&quot;. You can head to my MySpace page (<a href="http://www.myspace.com/djnphared" target="_blank" title="www.myspace.com/djnphared">www.myspace.com/DJNphared</a>) to hear an <u>EXCLUSIVE</u> track off the mixtape featuring <strong>Chali 2na</strong> and then you can proceed to (<a href="http://www.myspace.com/JPinder" target="_blank" title="www.myspace.com/JPinder">www.myspace.com/JPinder</a>) and check out a couple of tracks off his forthcoming album &quot;<u><strong>The Backpack Theory</strong></u>&quot;. Go in with open ears and open minds, you might not come out the same.<br /><br />And if you&#39;re jonesin&#39; to see the homie perform live, he&#39;s gonna be rocking the <strong>MF Doom</strong> show at the Showbox in Seattle on this coming Friday the 17th, and the next night he will be doing his thing at the <strong>Sportn&#39; Life Records 5 Year Anniversary Show</strong> at Seattle&#39;s own Chop Suey along with Seattle&#39;s finest in <strong>D.Black</strong>, <strong>Grynch</strong>, <strong>Cancer Rising</strong>  and <strong>Jake One</strong> on the cuts. I highly recommend you hit up both and make this weekend one to remember!<br /><br /><div style="text-align: center"><img src="http://i63.photobucket.com/albums/h145/d_black77/snl-fly-black-1.jpg" alt="" width="369" height="459" /></div><br /><br />-Npha<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-13 | Monday</font></strong><br><br>said it last week, sayin it again this week.<strong>&nbsp; new downloads every week foo</strong>.<br /><br />peep the upper right corner of your screeny screen screen.<br /><br /><br /><br /><br /><br /><br /><p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-11 | Saturday</font></strong><br><br>tuesday, we had the opportunity to hit San Diego on many fronts.  our folks <strong>Kid John</strong> and <strong>Zar</strong> from <strong><a href="http://www.pnoyapparel.com/">pnoyapparel</a></strong> hosted us and set up a few events for Blue Scholars to rock at.<br /><br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/daygo/sd1.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br /><br />our first stop was <a href="http://www.pnoyapparel.com/">AMFM Skate Shop</a>.  located on Garnet Avenue, AMFM is San Diego&#39;s premier boutique skate shop and carrier of fine goods from <a href="http://www.stussy.com/">Stussy, </a><a href="http://www.orisue.com/">Orisue</a>, and other brands coveted by those who consider themselves the bastions of coolness.  <br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/daygo/hats.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />there, we had a chance to meet <strong>Cathy</strong> and <strong>Jay</strong> (or was it Ray?) who held the shop down in the daytime.  <br /><br />the homie Will, aka <strong>DJ Ill Will</strong> came thru and set up a little sound system for the instore.  as soon as the speakers and turntables got set up, the googly-eyed and drooling bedroom-DJ teenagers crowded around and stared at the decks and mixers; very much like how a kid stares at purple drink, or how an ethnic stereotype stares at their food of choice.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/daygo/sd2.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />oh right, we&#39;re in california.  skratch DJ mecca.  so we got together and cut it up like it was 1999.  literally.  peace to Jay/Ray and young <strong>Ray</strong> (this other guy was actually named Ray) for holdin it down.  keep doin your thing maynes.<br /><br />then we did a show.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/daygo/sd3.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />then we posed for a picture.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/daygo/sd4.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />check out <strong>Bee</strong> wearing the new Artist Edition shirt scheduled for release in the Fall.  and no, that&#39;s not a Persian Max Headroom, that&#39;s me.  <br /><br />i was informed that <strong>The Armory</strong>, a legendary hip hop store in San Diego, used to be just down the street.  then i was informed to stop calling San Diego, &quot;San Diego&quot; and call it &quot;Day-go&quot; instead or else i&#39;d be kicked in the kneecap repeatedly.<br /><br />talks of The Armory then reminded me of this little gem,<br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/daygo/cactus.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />a flick of Valentino&#39;s Mexican Restaurant mascot that was taken awhile back when we were in Day-go before.  haha, that&#39;s supposed to be a cactus.<br /><br />after AMFM, we went with Zar to get Mexican food and headed out to <strong>Kava Lounge</strong> for night time show.  there, i found out that <strong>Mike</strong>, co-owner of AMFM, in addition to managing a skate team, also rapped.  what&#39;s more, is he rapped with a band.  they held it down.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/daygo/sd5.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />Kava Lounge kinda reminded me of <strong>LoFi</strong> down by the freeway in Seattle.  except that Kava Lounge had a stage.  LoFi doesn&#39;t have a stage.  Kava did.  that was mainly why i think i liked Kava Lounge better than LoFi.  you know, the stage thing.  there were like 300 people there, some local opening acts, and an Orisue clothing giveaway that never ended.  good work pnoyappco!<br /><br />after the show, we all hi-fived each other for a job well done, kinda like the Hi-Fivin&#39; White Guys from Almost Live except without the white guys.  then we went to eat at Denny&#39;s.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/daygo/sd6.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />Denny&#39;s was ok.  not great.  i think the best part was when Geo suggested that we play in the <strong>Food Olympics</strong> right as our check came.  <br /><br />invented by frat boys in the mid-seventies, the Food Olympics began as a series of games incorporated into the hazing rituals employed during rush.  it eventually became a cultural pastime among stoners and suburbanites and went on to find a home in the routines of tokenized comedians of color who make fun of white people.  <br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/daygo/sd7.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />the game chosen for this night involved the creation of a dish from anything edible on the table.  Bee, not being a fan of pork, grabbed all the sausage and bacon, combined it with hashbrowns and eggs,  and slid it between two pancakes to make some kind of rancid homemade Denny&#39;s McGriddle.  a bunch of people said &quot;ew.&quot;<br /><br />then this poor bastard ate it.<br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/daygo/sd8.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />more people said ew, and he didn&#39;t have to pay.<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-10 | Friday</font></strong><br><br><img src="http://farm2.static.flickr.com/1314/1073641852_10c532251a_o.jpg" alt="" width="430" height="288" /><br /><br /><img src="http://farm2.static.flickr.com/1047/1072781409_c8f41b2fa7_o.jpg" alt="" width="430" height="288" /><br /><br /><img src="http://farm2.static.flickr.com/1107/1072780913_14e1080fc5_o.jpg" alt="" width="430" height="288" /><br /><br />More <strong>FILTHY</strong> pics from last Wednesday&#39;s <strong>Knitting Factory</strong> show (<em>courtesy of Apollo Victoria</em>) can be found on the <strong><a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/masslinemedia/" target="_blank">Massline Flickr page</a></strong>!<br /><br />Peace<br />daps<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><center><div class="pagenumbers"><A HREF="archive.php?from=95">[&lt;&lt;]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=0">[1]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=5">[2]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=10">[3]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=15">[4]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=20">[5]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=25">[6]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=30">[7]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=35">[8]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=40">[9]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=45">[10]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=50">[11]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=55">[12]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=60">[13]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=65">[14]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=70">[15]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=75">[16]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=80">[17]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=85">[18]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=90">[19]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=95">[20]</A> [21] <A HREF="archive.php?from=105">[22]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=110">[23]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=115">[24]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=120">[25]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=125">[26]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=130">[27]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=135">[28]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=140">[29]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=145">[30]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=150">[31]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=155">[32]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?f